{
  "description": "Shared CRC-32C (Castagnoli) vectors for the Fastio upload integrity contract. Copied verbatim to every client implementation; keep language-neutral.",
  "encoding": "All CRCs are lowercase 8-hex strings in standard big-endian form (crc32c(\"123456789\") = e3069283). Byte data is lowercase hex in *_hex fields. Lengths are decimal byte counts and may exceed 2^53 (len2 = 2^62 + 5): parse them as 64-bit integers or big integers.",
  "combine_contract": "combine(crc1, crc2, len2) is the CRC-32C of A||B given crc1 = crc32c(A), crc2 = crc32c(B) and len2 = byte length of B (zlib crc32_combine shape): reflected polynomial 0x82F63B78; len2 <= 0 returns crc1 unchanged; the CRC of empty input is 00000000. combine_ordered folds pieces left to right starting from 00000000.",
  "derivation": "Generated by an independent Python 3 program. compute is a bit-at-a-time reflected CRC-32C (init 0xFFFFFFFF, xorout 0xFFFFFFFF, poly 0x82F63B78), and every compute, combine and combine_ordered expected value is the CRC-32C of the concatenated bytes (direct oracle). combine_synthetic lengths cannot be materialised, so their expected values come from zlib 1.2.11 GF(2) 32x32 operator-matrix squaring, a different algorithm from the x^(8*len2) mod P form the helpers implement; that matrix method was itself checked against the concatenation oracle on every combine case above. associativity_synthetic holds under the same method: combine(combine(crc1, crc2, len2), crc3, len3) == combine(crc1, combine(crc2, crc3, len3), len2 + len3) == expected.",
